In this session we discussed ethics, risk, responsibility, and governance in AI product management. I agree with what we discussed, bias may not even be intentional and one other thing is there are so may factors that connect with bias like historical data, societal prejudices, labeling etc. AI models trained on biased data will produce biased results. Like we say garbage in, garbage out but this can happen even without our knowledge. So assuming that our data is correct and there is absolutely no room for bias is not something we should follow blindly. We have to be actively aware of hidden biases and try to actively construct ways to check and correct them. This is very important across domains like healthcare, legal, finance etc.
I learnt that we should always speak in terms of the problem or product at hand. Fairness, privacy, and safety must be defined within the boundaries of our specific product and customer base. We do the same thing for evaluating patient safety. Generalized guidelines must be adapted into actionable measures that work on the ground.
Another key takeaway for me is the role of PM to protect user privacy and minimize exposure. Healthcare has very strict rules around data privacy, consent and usage. I could relate to this well. 
We also discussed hallucinations, RAG, and verification of AI generated insights. We have to understand that AI is also a tool. We cannot outsource accountability to AI. That has to strictly be under the realm of a human/committee of people. Launching a product is only the beginning. We must be adept at continuous monitoring, audits, and open to corrections and reworks to ensure that our product is successful and most importantly useful to intended people.
